grayscale is adding solana to one of  their funds now what is so fascinating  about this is it took years from 2014 i  believe is when grayscale got launched  it took years to get institutional  investors interested in bitcoin to get  them into these institutional quality  funds whether it was gbtc uh in a  publicly traded trust or in some sort of  private company but or a private fund  now grayscale is quickly quickly quickly  adding new types of assets solana  uniswap etc they've added it to their  digital large cap fund which has about  494 million about 500 million dollars in  it there is a a fun component breakdown  that i believe we have and when you  start to look at this uh list of assets  these are not the assets that you would  think that institutions would likely be  buying but to have 500 million dollars  in the fund is way more than i would  have thought so we see everything from  bitcoin cardano uniswap litecoin  ethereum solana chain link bitcoin cash  etc when you have a large entity like  grayscale which manages 30 40 billion  dollars of assets and they begin to add  the long tail to it their institutional  clients whether people like it or not  they are going to start to expand what  they're investing in from bitcoin to  other types of assets we know that they  have a bitcoin trust they have an  ethereum trust they also have other  types of individual asset trusts but  what you see here is a digital large cap  fund now it's not surprising because  something like a solana wasn't in the  top ten then it goes into the top ten if  you look at a company like bitwise where  i'm an investor they essentially create  passive indexes so they have something  called the bitwise 10. now what is the  bitwise 10 it's the 10 largest cap  assets on a market weight basis and they  rebalance it on a periodic basis and so  naturally what you're going to get is  you're going to have long only single  asset investors i buy bitcoin i hold it  for a long period of time uh those at  those are the investors that have  dominated the market for a long time  then we started to get active traders  people who would come in and they would  buy and sell some day trading some a  little bit less periodic but still the  ability to buy low sell high and do that  over and over and over again now what  we're seeing is a rise in more and more  passive vehicles so things like top 10  of the market cap or certain sectors  similar to what we see in the etf or the  passive index space and so if it happens  in the legacy market it's going to  happen in this market as well we've got  a quote here from grayskills head of  legal it says we will sell the existing  fund components in proportion to their  ratings and then put in solana and  unison the fund is really a great way  for our investors who don't want to make  a targeted bet on bitcoin ethereum or  another digital asset they just really  want broad market exposure and so what  we are likely to see is some of the  capital that's going to come into the  market they no longer want to play  security selection right they simply  just say i want to have crypto exposure  whether we like it or we don't we think  it's positive or negative those  investors are going to demand  broad-based  exposure in an index and so naturally if  there is demand companies are going to  build these types of products do we  think that this is a positive or a  negative for the industry and are we  surprised that more and more people want  broad-based index exposure  no i don't think so i don't think we  should be surprised i think it uh it  limits your downside a little bit right  like on the risk curve if you look at it  uh kind of having exposure to everything  in aggregate sure maybe you should have  conviction on kind of one or two assets  that you really believe in but if your  if your general idea is that these  assets are going to all appreciate in  value and there's too many of them for  you to really pick and choose from and  you don't know which ones are going to  be the winners and you just want a  little exposure to the market and the  upside of kind of the asymmetry that  this uh kind of asset class offers then  sure i think it's uh it's probably a  smart decision to be able to invest in a  vehicle like this john what do you think  it's a no-brainer i don't see it being a  negative to the industry at all same  thing as uh almost like an etf in my  mind where you get that exposure across  just the entire asset class  i uh i'm personally bullish real big on  bitcoin so having the other ones in  there um i don't own any of this but  it's interesting to see and i can see  why people rush to it and why uh they  are constantly adding and removing  things based on the market size and uh  the market cap of the different assets  i think if anything it's good for solana  and uniswap right well yeah  like outside of kind of just like  industry looking trends and all that  kind of stuff like it's obviously good  for these uh kind of these individual  assets to be included in the broad base  index  the other piece of it is  when i think about the broad base  indexes is i actually have more  confidence that investors allocating to  those indexes are unlikely to sell  because they're literally just looking  over a long period of time let me invest  into a lower cost index just give me  kind of the industry and they're not  going to worry so much about what one  individual asset goes up or down in  price versus if i do security selection  right or the idea of kind of a single  asset selection if i buy a bitcoin or  buy ethereum then all of a sudden i  start to get in the mindset of like oh i  bought bitcoin uh but maybe uh wait it  went down ten percent this other one  went up maybe i should switch or i  bought another one oh that one went down  this one went up i should switch and so  the index type exposure gives me hope  and again we'll see that they're longer  term stronger hands which ultimately is  a greater tailwind for the entire  industry yeah and i think uh kind of to  your point right is like if you just  want there's certainly investors uh  whether they're kind of institutional  investors or retail investors that just  want one two three four five percent  exposure into the industry right they  don't want to put all their capital at  work uh in digital assets they want a  bunch of other stocks a bunch of other  stuff whether it's stocks bonds etc  and they just want that little bit of  exposure to get off zero right and i  think this is a vehicle that you could  quickly see why they would invest into  it uh so they don't have to go out and  pick a certain asset and say hey look  this is gonna be the winner i'm gonna  put all my percent in this or kind of  all my capital in this instead i'm gonna  go over here and get broad-based  exposure to the industry and hope over  the next five 10 15 years that it  appreciates in value and it's a small  part of their uh portfolio in general so  yeah to your point they're not gonna go  out and be actively day trading this  stuff they're not gonna be selling it  all the time they're gonna let it sit  there and see if it appreciates overtime
 

